; The float set at its edge: what each fp32 OpenCL.std instruction does at a zero, an
; infinity, a NaN, a subnormal and the end of the exponent range, on both columns.
;
; It runs under `--exact`, which compares the two buffers as words rather than as
; numbers, and it has to: `max |diff|` cannot see a NaN, because a NaN difference is
; not greater than anything, and it cannot see the sign of a zero, because
; 0 - (-0) is 0. Every column writes the result itself, no test, no select, no
; sentinel, so a payload, an infinity, a signed zero or a subnormal that one column
; produces and the other does not is a difference in the words.
;
; What it covers is the part a tolerance hides: 1/0 and -1/0 and 0/0, 0*inf,
; inf-inf, fmax+fmax, sqrt of -1 and of -0, (-0)+(-0), log of 0 and of -1, exp past
; the range, tgamma at a pole, sin of an infinity, fdim and minmag and maxmag at
; their edges, nextafter at the top of the range and off both zeros, fmod and
; remainder by zero, powr of a negative base, rootn of a negative radicand at both
; parities, and ldexp past both ends. The five that widen to the fp64 text get their
; edges too, `erf` of an infinity, `erfc` of -1, `cbrt` of -0, `hypot` of an
; infinity, `log1p` of -0, beside `acosh` below its domain, `asinh` of -infinity and
; `exp10` past the range.
;
; Fifteen of the columns are the five instructions that answer twice. Each takes a
; pointer for its second value, so the module carries the same two buffers the sweep
; does: a2 for the `float` halves and a3 for the integers. A `frexp` that disagrees
; about the exponent of the smallest subnormal, a `modf` that loses the sign of
; -0.0's whole part, a `sincos` whose cosine of an infinity is a number rather than
; a NaN, a `remquo` whose quotient of a zero divisor is a number, or a `fract` whose
; floor is the wrong number, is a difference in the words. `fract` is the fifth of
; them: instruction 30 returns `fmin(x - floor(x), 0x1.fffffep-1f)` and writes
; `floor(x)` through its pointer, so it is asked at an infinity, at -0.0, at 1e30, at
; 1.5 and at -3, and both halves of each answer are compared.
               OpCapability Kernel
               OpCapability Addresses
               OpCapability Int64
       %ocl = OpExtInstImport "OpenCL.std"
               OpMemoryModel Physical64 OpenCL
               OpEntryPoint Kernel %main "f32edge" %gid
               OpName %main "f32edge"
               OpDecorate %gid BuiltIn GlobalInvocationId
      %void = OpTypeVoid
       %u64 = OpTypeInt 64 0
       %u32 = OpTypeInt 32 0
       %f32 = OpTypeFloat 32
      %f32p = OpTypePointer CrossWorkgroup %f32
      %u32p = OpTypePointer CrossWorkgroup %u32
     %v3u64 = OpTypeVector %u64 3
       %pin = OpTypePointer Input %v3u64
      %fnty = OpTypeFunction %void %f32p %f32p %f32p %u32p
        %gid = OpVariable %pin Input
      %pzero = OpConstant %f32 0
      %nzero = OpConstant %f32 -0
        %one = OpConstant %f32 1
      %n_one = OpConstant %f32 -1
       %half = OpConstant %f32 0.5
    %onehalf = OpConstant %f32 1.5
    %n_three = OpConstant %f32 -3
    %n_eight = OpConstant %f32 -8
        %big = OpConstant %f32 1e30
       %fmax = OpConstant %f32 3.4028234663852886e+38
        %sub = OpConstant %f32 0x0.000002p-126
        %c1i = OpConstant %u32 1
        %c2i = OpConstant %u32 2
        %c3i = OpConstant %u32 3
    %cM150i = OpConstant %u32 4294967146
       %cNu = OpConstant %u64 53
      %c00u = OpConstant %u64 0
      %c01u = OpConstant %u64 1
      %c02u = OpConstant %u64 2
      %c03u = OpConstant %u64 3
      %c04u = OpConstant %u64 4
      %c05u = OpConstant %u64 5
      %c06u = OpConstant %u64 6
